# gdlint configuration. Run via `make lint`.
# Only the rules that deviate from gdtoolkit's defaults are listed here;
# dump the full default set with `gdlint --dump-default-config`.

# Declaration-ordering inside a class. Disabled deliberately: it fires 27 times
# across the presentation layer (@onready vars declared beside the plain vars
# they relate to, `enum State` sitting next to the consts it indexes), and
# satisfying it means reshuffling declarations for no correctness benefit.
disable:
- class-definitions-order

# Command `validate()` methods are guard-clause validators — one early return
# per rejection reason, each with its own error string (attack_command has 9).
# That shape is required by the command pattern in CLAUDE.md; collapsing it into
# nested conditionals would be strictly worse. 12 leaves room without inviting
# sprawl.
max-returns: 12

# Ratchet, not a target, in the same spirit as max-file-lines below:
# scenes/battle/battle_view.gd is the widest class and sits at exactly 21 — it is
# the board's rendering facade, so each method is one thing the scene asks it to
# draw (a sprite, an overlay, the fog pass, the HUD). It went 20 -> 21 when
# `_restage_identity` became `restage_identity`: the scenario driver was already
# calling it across the class boundary, and a public seam is the honest spelling
# of a caller another class has. Adding to BattleView means moving something out
# first, which is the point.
#
# It went 21 -> 22 for `hud_chip_rect`, the one owner of the corner the floating
# commander chip occupied, and back down to 21 when the docked HUD bars retired
# both: there is no floating chip left for a menu to dodge, so the geometry seam
# came out with it. Adding to BattleView still means moving something out first.
max-public-methods: 21

# Ratchet, not a target: scenes/battle/battle.gd is the longest file and is
# being split a responsibility at a time. Set at its current length so the file
# cannot grow without tripping the lint, and lowered each time it shrinks — 1320
# when it held the demo driver, the whole rendering layer, and every animation;
# 920 once BattleScenarioDriver, BattleView, BattleAnimator, and BattleSetup had
# moved out; 863 once BattleAiRunner (the computer's side of the interaction
# flow) had too, making room for the commander battle-identity HUD; 825 now that
# BattleMenus owns what each menu offers, which is where the production
# facilities air units introduced would otherwise have landed; 820 once the four
# plain unit actions — Load, Supply, Dive, Wait — collapsed into the one commit
# helper they had each been spelling out; 815 once the day banner stopped being
# a twelve-line formatted expression, which paid for the victory guard.
#
# It went back up to 918 because rebasing the game-speed work onto the balance
# lab landed two independent additions in battle.gd at once: watch mode on one
# side — the planner dictionary, the day cap and the watched-result report — and
# the speed picker on the other, with its capture pin, merge fade and speed
# action. Neither side is loose, and splitting battle.gd is not that change's
# job. The ceiling tracks battle.gd itself again rather than
# tools/run_commander_balance.gd, which briefly held the limit while it was the
# longer file and now sits at 788 after coming apart on the balance branch.
# battle.gd sits at exactly 918: adding to it means moving something else out
# first, which is the point — and the number comes back down the moment the
# scene sheds a responsibility.
#
# 905 once BattleZoom — the camera zoom level, its clamp against the view and the
# keyboard zoom steps — moved out, which is where the small-map camera work (a
# new min_zoom that lets a small board zoom out until it fits) would otherwise
# have landed.
#
# The ceiling then bound scenes/menu/main_menu.gd, which passed battle.gd as the
# longest file when the menu grew its 640x360 capture gate: the apparatus lives
# in MenuCaptureDriver rather than the menu because inlining it went over this
# limit. Same rule on every file — adding means moving something out first.
#
# 905 -> 938 for scenes/battle/battle_scenario_driver.gd, which passed
# main_menu.gd in turn: the COM-18 legibility gate staged four more HUD scenarios
# (charging, ready on a bright board, AI, mirror) plus the map-menu route and the
# rect check that keeps that menu clear of the opaque chrome. The driver is a list
# of staged boards by nature, so it grows a scenario at a time; it is the next file
# due to come apart.
#
# 938 -> 940 and the ceiling is main_menu.gd's again, for the same reason it
# once went back up to 918: rebasing landed two independent additions at once.
# The scenario driver took the limit to 938 on one side; on the other, every
# native tooltip_text in the menu became an anchored Tooltip, which is a wider
# call at each of the seven sites (a label, a detail line and a side, where
# there had been one string) plus the tip-label helper the segmented groups
# share. The component itself is already out of the file, in
# scenes/ui/tooltip.gd — what is left here is the call sites, and they are the
# menu's own. Neither side is loose, and splitting main_menu.gd is not the
# tooltip change's job. It sits at exactly 940.
#
# 940 -> 986 and the ceiling is the scenario driver's again, for COM-16: the map
# menu grew the two routes out of a running match, and `leave_confirm` walks them
# — the rows on the menu, the confirmation the unsaved one opens, and both rects
# against the board band. Battle itself did not pay for that raise; BattleExit
# came out of it in the same change, and battle.gd ends shorter than it started
# (908). The driver is still the file due to come apart, and the shape of the
# split has not moved: the cut-in family — both `_stage_*_cut_in`, the two skip
# walks, the pair staging and the atlas-row checks — is a third of the file and
# answers to one idea. It sits at exactly 986.
#
# 986 -> 998, the scenario driver's again and the same scenario's: review asked
# `leave_confirm` to guard the reset_size() half of COM-16 too, since a stale
# panel behind the confirmation passes the band check unchanged. The guard is the
# map menu's height measured before the row is chosen and compared against the
# confirmation's. It sits at exactly 998.
#
# 998 -> 1069, the scenario driver's a fourth time, for COM-12's two mission-strip
# scenarios: the opening frame a fresh install meets, and the one that walks a
# whole first turn — select, move, build, end turn — and then checks the strip has
# advanced to the step nobody performed. That is the acceptance criterion a still
# frame cannot show, and ending the turn is the rule with the most to get wrong,
# since the computer plays through the same events and must not retire the
# player's hint. Seventy-one lines for two staged boards, the turn walk, and the
# hint-pin predicate they read; the band check `leave_confirm` already had was
# generalised from "the action menu" to "this control" rather than copied, which
# is the only reason it is not more. The split named above is still the one that
# is due and its shape has still not moved: the cut-in family is a third of this
# file and answers to one idea. It sits at exactly 1069.
#
# 1069 -> 1113, the scenario driver's a fifth time, for COM-11: the ghost panel
# the 998 raise guarded on the map menu is the same defect on the build menu,
# and no scenario walked that order. `after_build_menu` opens the eleven-row
# shop, cancels, opens a two-row unit menu and measures it against the one before
# it on both axes — the sequence is the whole check, since either menu on its own
# already photographs correctly. The split these notes have now named several
# times is still the right one and still not this change's job: the cut-in
# family is a third of the file and twelve smoke scenarios rest on it, so moving
# it inside a ghost-panel fix would leave neither half reviewable. It is filed
# as COM-94. The driver sits at exactly 1113.
max-file-lines: 1113
